<sn><md>### Git editor

This is a completely different concept.

When Git needs you to write text, it launches your configured editor.

For example:

```bash
git commit
```

Git may open an editor asking you to enter:

```text
Merge branch 'ui' into main

# Please enter a commit message...
```

Similarly:

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~5
```

may open an editor containing:

```text
pick abc123 first commit
pick def456 second commit
pick ghi789 third commit
```

This is **not file comparison**.

You can configure an editor such as Sublime Text:

```bash
git config --global core.editor "subl -n -w"
```

Then:

```bash
git commit
```

will open the commit message in Sublime.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### `git diff`

Compares two versions and prints the changes in the terminal.

```bash
git diff
```

For example:

```text
- old code
+ new code
```

You can compare branches:

```bash
git diff main..ui
```

Or specific files:

```bash
git diff main..ui -- App.js
```

It **doesn't modify anything**.
</md></sn><sn><md>### `git difftool`

Does essentially the same comparison as `git diff`, but sends the files to a configured visual diff application.

For example, with Sublime Merge:

```bash
git difftool main..ui
```

Instead of seeing:

```text
- old line
+ new line
```

you get a visual comparison such as:

```text
┌──────────────┬──────────────┐
│     main     │      ui      │
│              │              │
│ old code     │ new code     │
└──────────────┴──────────────┘
```

**Think:**

&gt; `git diff` = textual comparison
&gt; `git difftool` = graphical comparison
</md></sn><sn><md>### `git mergetool`

This is different.

Suppose you have:

```bash
git merge ui
```

and Git reports:

```text
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in App.js
```

Now you have something like:

```text
&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD
code from main
=======
code from ui
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; ui
```

`git mergetool` opens a tool such as Sublime Merge to help you **resolve the conflict**.

Conceptually:

```text
             MERGE CONFLICT

       main              ui
        │                 │
        ▼                 ▼
   ┌─────────┐       ┌─────────┐
   │ version │       │ version │
   └────┬────┘       └────┬────┘
        │                 │
        └────────┬────────┘
                 ▼
          ┌─────────────┐
          │   MERGED    │
          │   VERSION   │
          └─────────────┘
```

Unlike `git difftool`, **mergetool is intended to produce the final merged file**.
</md></sn><sn><md>### The important distinction

Think of Git operations like this:

```text
                 GIT
                  │
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       │          │          │
       ▼          ▼          ▼
    COMPARE     MERGE      WRITE TEXT
       │          │          │
       ▼          ▼          ▼
   git diff   git mergetool  editor
       │          │          │
       ▼          ▼          ▼
   terminal    Sublime      Sublime
                  │
                  ▼
             resolve conflict
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Set submile_merge as diff.tool and merge.tool

On **Ubuntu**, you can configure Sublime Merge as both Git's **difftool** and **mergetool**.

First check `smerge`

```bash
which smerge
```

If it returns:

```text
/usr/bin/smerge
```

you're ready.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 1. Configure Sublime Merge as `difftool`

```bash
git config --global diff.tool sublime
git config --global difftool.sublime.cmd 'smerge mergetool "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"'
```

Test it:

```bash
git difftool
```

For comparing branches:

```bash
git difftool main..ui
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 3. Configure Sublime Merge as `mergetool`

```bash
git config --global merge.tool sublime
git config --global mergetool.sublime.cmd 'smerge mergetool "$BASE" "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE" -o "$MERGED"'
```

Test during a merge conflict:

```bash
git mergetool
```

Sublime Merge should open the conflict resolution interface.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Verify both

```bash
git config --global --list | grep -E 'diff.tool|difftool|merge.tool|mergetool'
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
diff.tool=sublime
difftool.sublime.cmd=smerge mergetool "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"
merge.tool=sublime
mergetool.sublime.cmd=smerge mergetool "$BASE" "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE" -o "$MERGED"
```
**verify editor**

```
git config --global core.editor
```

</md></sn><sn><md>#### One important note

The two commands have different purposes:

```text
git difftool
      ↓
Compare two versions
      ↓
LOCAL vs REMOTE
```

while:

```text
git mergetool
      ↓
Resolve a conflict
      ↓
BASE + LOCAL + REMOTE → MERGED
```

So **don't use the same command configuration for both**. The `difftool` needs `$LOCAL` and `$REMOTE`, while the `mergetool` also needs `$BASE` and `$MERGED`.
</md></sn>